-- What somebody actually does with allium-inspect, written as demands on it.
--
-- These are journeys against the tool's own specification, which makes them
-- doubly useful and slightly vertiginous: walking them exercises the journey
-- engine, and what they report is a statement about the spec of the thing
-- doing the reporting.

-- The whole premise, in five steps.
--
-- Somebody has been handed a specification they did not write. Everything this
-- tool does is in service of the next twenty minutes of that person's life.
journey SomebodyMeetsASpecTheyDidNotWrite {
    goal: Ines has been asked to review a spec set she has never opened. She
          points the tool at it, sees what it holds, picks a construct that
          looks load-bearing, and reads the line the author actually wrote —
          without opening an editor or knowing which file to open.

    -- The ways this journey should be shown. Declared rather than left to
    -- whatever the harness happens to emit: a declaration is a demand, so the
    -- panel offers the control before a picture exists and says which of these
    -- nothing has answered yet. A tag outside it is a typo worth being told
    -- about rather than a second axis nobody meant.
    shows:
        theme: dark, light

    cast:
        ines:      Reader
        person:    Person
        construct: Construct

    given:
        person.role = reader

    1. she points at the directory
        ines does SomebodyPointsAtASpecSet(ines, "./specs") on SetOfFiles
            creating set: SpecSet
        then ReadTheSet fires
        then set.status = reading

    2. it finishes reading, and says how much there is
        -- `module_count` is derived from the modules themselves, and this
        -- simulator does not compute derived values — so the rule waiting on it
        -- can never be woken without saying what it is. Said here, once, and
        -- printed in the ledger above the walk.
        stipulate set.module_count = 4
        stipulate set.parsed = true
        -- A state-condition rule is not fired by anybody; it becomes true or it
        -- does not. Letting a moment pass is how a journey says "and then
        -- whatever had become true, happened".
        after 1.second
        then FinishReading fires
        then set.status = read
        ines sees set.module_count on SetOfFiles

    3. she opens it in a browser
        ines does SomebodyOpensTheBrowser(ines, set) on Canvas
            creating session: Session
        then OpenTheBrowser fires
        then session.looking_at = domain

    4. she picks something that looks load-bearing
        ines does SomebodyPicksAConstruct(ines, session, construct) on Canvas
            creating selection: Selection
        then SelectAConstruct fires
        then selection.showing_source = true

    5. and reads what the author wrote, not a tidied version of it
        ines sees selection.construct on Canvas

    ends: She has read a declaration in its own words, having started from a
          directory name and nothing else.
}

-- The loop the tool is built around: save the file, watch the picture move.
--
-- Nobody presses reload. If they have to, the tool has failed at the one thing
-- that makes it worth leaving open beside an editor.
journey AnEditReachesTheReaderWithoutBeingAskedAbout {
    goal: Priya has the tool open on a spec while she edits it. She saves, and
          the picture in front of her catches up on its own — and until it has,
          it says so rather than showing her a file that no longer says that.

    cast:
        priya:   Author
        ines:    Reader
        person:  Person
        set:     SpecSet
        module:  Module

    given:
        person.role = author
        module.source_readable = true
        module.owner = set
        set.status = read
        set.revision = 1
        set.dirty = false

    1. somebody is already reading it
        ines does SomebodyOpensTheBrowser(ines, set) on Canvas
            creating session: Session
        then session.stale = false

    2. she saves a file
        priya does AuthorSavesAFile(priya, module) on TheFilesOnDisk
        then NoticeAChange fires

    3. the set reads itself again, and the revision moves
        after 1.second
        then BeginReread fires
        then module.owner.status = reading

    4. the picture admits it is behind
        after 1.second
        then NoticeTheSetChanged fires
        then session.stale = true

    5. and then catches up
        after 1.second
        then CatchUp fires
        then session.stale = false

    ends: The reader never asked for any of that, which is the point.
}

-- Narrowing to one file, which is the question the Modules view exists for.
journey NarrowingToOneFileStillShowsWhatItNeeds {
    goal: Tomas wants to know what one module actually depends on. He switches
          the others off — and the references that leave the file end on the
          files they went to, so the one he is looking at never looks
          self-contained when it is not.

    cast:
        tomas:  Reader
        person: Person
        set:    SpecSet
        other:  Module

    given:
        person.role = reader
        set.status = read

    1. he opens the set
        tomas does SomebodyOpensTheBrowser(tomas, set) on Canvas
            creating session: Session
        then OpenTheBrowser fires

    2. he switches another file off
        tomas does SomebodySwitchesAModuleOff(tomas, session, other) on Canvas
            creating hidden: HiddenModule
        then HideAModule fires

    3. and can still see how much that file was carrying
        tomas sees other.exported on Canvas

    ends: What was hidden is still accounted for, rather than silently absent.
}
